Systems and Programs
•	IMIS
o	Long Form: Integrated Maintenance Information System
o	Meaning: The primary ground-based software system used by maintainers. It's the "system of record" for all maintenance actions, work orders, part installations, and troubleshooting. The XML files you receive are extracts from this system.
•	AEFR
o	Long Form: Advanced Engine Fault Resolution
o	Meaning: An "expert system" or software module that resides within IMIS. Its job is to analyze fault data downloaded from the engine and provide maintainers with a ranked list of probable causes and recommended troubleshooting steps.
•	CeduLoad
o	Long Form: Central Engine Diagnostic Unit Loader (or a close variant)
o	Meaning: This is a ground-based software program. Its specific function is to take the large, raw, binary data files recorded by the engine's data acquisition system during a flight and decode them into human-readable text files (like Cedu_Engine_Health.txt) that can then be loaded into the database.
•	EHM
o	Long Form: Engine Health Management
o	Meaning: A broad, overarching discipline or philosophy. It refers to the entire ecosystem of technology, software (like HAMR, IMIS), and processes used to monitor, diagnose, and maintain the health of the engine fleet.
•	CEMS
o	Long Form: Comprehensive Engine Management System
o	Meaning: A high-level logistics and asset management system, likely used at the Air Force or command level. While IMIS is for the "hands-on" maintainer, CEMS tracks the location, status, and overall lifecycle of every engine across the entire fleet. The CONFIG.xlsx spreadsheet is a report generated from CEMS.
•	ADEM
o	Long Form: Advanced Data Extraction and Management (or a similar program name)
o	Meaning: Your notes mention it in relation to CUST (customers). This is likely the name of the program or project that owns or manages the database you are working with. It's the "customer" for this data warehouse.
